Implementing a multi-tree list in an Angular application can be done using the `MatTreeGrid` component from Angular Material if you're looking for a more flexible and powerful solution than just plain HTML lists or other tree components.
Here's a step-by-step guide on how to create a multi-tree list with Angular:

  1. First, install the necessary dependencies: MatTreeGrid (if you want to use it) and any other third-party libraries that may be needed.

  2. Create your model classes representing the data structure of your multi-tree list. For example:
```typescript
export class TreeNode {
    public id: string;
    public name: string;
    public children?: TreeNode[];
}
```

  3. In your AppComponent or wherever your multi-tree list will appear, import the MatTreeGrid module and add it to your imports array in the AppModule:
```typescript
import { MatTreeGridModule } from '@angular/material/tree-grid';
@NgModule({
  declarations: [AppComponent],
  imports: [
    // ...
    MatTreeGridModule,
  ],
  bootstrap: [AppComponent]
})
export class AppModule {}
```

  4. Now, let's say you have a collection of nodes represented as an array of TreeNode objects. You can initialize a MatTreeGrid instance like this:
```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    <mat-treegrid #treeGrid>
      <!-- Define columns here -->
    </mat-treegrid>
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  private treeData: TreeNode[] = [
    // Your node data goes here...
  ];

  constructor(private matTreeGrid: MatTreeGridComponent<TreeNode>) {}

  ngOnInit(): void {
    this.matTreeGrid.setDataSource(this.treeData);
  }
}
```

  5. If you don't need to customize the appearance or behavior of the tree grid, you can skip steps 6-9 and go straight to step 10.
  6. To define columns in the tree grid, you'll need to create an interface that represents each column's properties and values. For example:
```typescript
interface Column {
  headerText: string; // The header text for this column
  property: keyof TreeNode; // The property of TreeNode that corresponds to this column's value
}

const columns: Column[] = [
  { headerText: 'Name', property: 'name' },
  { headerText: 'ID', property: 'id' },
];
```

  7. In your template file, you can then bind these columns to the appropriate properties of the tree grid:
```html
<mat-treegrid #treeGrid [dataSource]="treeData">
  <ng-template matColumnDef="name">  
    <div>{{ item.name }}</div> 
  </ng-template>

  <ng-template matColumnDef="id">
    <div>{{ item.id }}</div>
  </ng-template>

  <!-- Add all your columns here -->

</mat-treegrid>
```

  8. If you need to modify the appearance of the tree grid, such as changing its color scheme or adding hover effects, you can do so by creating custom styles or modifying existing ones.
  9. Finally, make sure to export your AppComponent and AppModule files so they can be imported into your project.

That should give you a basic idea of how to create a multi-tree list in an Angular application. Of course, there are many ways to customize the look and feel of the tree grid depending on your needs. You can also extend the functionality beyond what is provided by default by using other features available in Angular Material, such as sorting, filtering, and pagination.

