System.out.println(i); // This might throw InputMismatchException
```

### 5. Review File/InputStreams:
- If reading from files or other streams, check if the file exists or the stream has reached its end correctly.

By following these steps, you should be able to identify and resolve the `InputMismatchException`. Make sure to replace `"your_file.txt"` with the actual path to your file when using the `FileReader` approach.

To blend different audio files and create a realistic summer day experience using Java, you can use the Open Source library called AudioSystem. This library allows you to play back audio files on your computer. Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to load two audio files - one for the natural sound and another for the artificial sound.
```java
import javax.sound.sampled.*;
public class SummerDayAudio {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws LineUnavailableException {
        // Load the natural sound
        File naturalSoundFile = new File("path/to/natural_sound.wav");
        Clip clipNatural = AudioSystem.getClip();
        clipNatural.open(AudioFormat.Encoding.PCM_SIGNED, 
                new ShortData(new DataLine.Info(Clip.class,
                        AudioFormat.Encoding.PCM_SIGNED)),
                0);
        clipNatural.setFramePosition(0);
        clipNatural.start();

        // Load the artificial sound
        File artificialSoundFile = new File("path/to/artificial_sound.wav");
        Clip clipArtificial = AudioSystem.getLine(
                new Line.Info(Line.Type.INPUT));
        clipArtificial.open(AudioFormat.Encoding.PCM_SIGNED, 
                new ShortData(new DataLine.Info(Clip.class,
                        AudioFormat.Encoding.PCM_SIGNED)));
        clipArtificial.addLineListener(new LineListener() {
            @Override
            public void update(LineEvent event) {
                if (event.getType().equals(LineEvent.Type.START)) {
                    // Start adding artificial sound
                    clipArtificial.start();
                }
            }
        });
        clipArtificial.setFramePosition(0);
        clipArtificial.addLineListener(new LineListener() {
            @Override
            public void update(LineEvent event) {
                if (event.getType().equals(LineEvent.Type.STOP)) {
                    // Stop adding artificial sound
                    clipArtificial.stop();
                }
            }
        });

        // Play both clips simultaneously
        while (true) {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(100); // Wait for 100 milliseconds before switching clips
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {}
            if (clipArtificial.isActive()) {
                clipArtificial.stop(); // Stop artificial sound when it finishes
                clipNatural.stop(); // Stop natural sound once artificial sound stops
            }
            clipArtificial.start(); // Start artificial sound again
            clipNatural.start(); // Start natural sound again
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we first open two `Clip` objects representing our natural and artificial sound files. We then start playing each clip separately using their respective `start()` methods.
To add artificial sounds to enhance the overall experience, we added a `LineListener` that listens for changes in the line state (i.e., whether a microphone input is available). When the listener detects that a microphone input is available, it starts playing the artificial sound using the `play()` method of the `Clip` object.
Similarly, when the listener detects that there is no longer a microphone input available (i.e., the artificial sound has finished playing), it stops playing the artificial sound using the `stop()` method.
Finally, we loop through all the necessary steps repeatedly until the program exits. This ensures that the natural and artificial sounds alternate seamlessly throughout the duration of the program.
